on-arrival: {!
    Q.vox_active = true;
    for (var c of Q.congreso_constituencies) {
        if (c === 'navarra') continue; // Navarra right runs via PP/UPN/NSuma dynamics
        var pp = Q['pp_congreso_' + c + '_support'] || 0;
        var drain = Math.min(8.0, pp * 0.22);
        Q['pp_congreso_' + c + '_support'] = pp - drain;
        Q['vox_congreso_' + c + '_support'] = (Q['vox_congreso_' + c + '_support'] || 0) + drain;
    }
!}
role: event

= A new far right?

From the depths of the Spanish political spectrum, Vox seems to be climbing up the polls ever so steadily, and now we're reaching a point where they may very well get an important share of the seats in the Congreso. With a heavy dose of nostalgia for the Franco-era Spain and tubro-charged chauvinistic nationalism, Vox is openly demanding the abolition of the autonomy-based State in favour of re-centralization, the mass deportation of immigrants, a ban on any party or organization seeking independence from Spain, and cuts to women and LGBTQ+ rights, among many others.

Set to challenge the supremacy of the PSOE in regions like Andalusia by achieving tactical alliances with the PP, the threat is no longer only theoretical, and has put many in high alert. It seems like the new reactionary, far-right wave has finally reached Spain.

- @post_event: Worrying, to say the least
